The interception of requests in an SSO setup is primarily achieved using application-layer mechanisms (HTTP redirects, proxies, agents), not "DNS magic" in the way one might think of re-routing all traffic at the network level. DNS's role is foundational (translating domain names to IP addresses), but the logic of authentication interception happens at the HTTP layer. 
How SSO Interception is Achieved
The core mechanisms for interception rely on managing the HTTP request/response flow:
Proxies and Gateways (Recommended Approach):
A dedicated proxy server sits in front of the application server.
All user requests go to the proxy first.
The proxy inspects the request. If the user is unauthenticated, the proxy issues an HTTP 302 Redirect to the SSO server's login URL.
After successful authentication at the SSO server, the user's browser is redirected back to the proxy with an authentication token (e.g., SAML assertion, OIDC token).
The proxy validates the token, establishes a session, and forwards the original request to the internal application, potentially adding necessary HTTP headers with user identity information.
Web Agents/Filters (In-Application Approach):
A software module (agent) is installed directly within the web application server (e.g., an Apache module or Java Servlet filter).
This agent intercepts requests at the application level.
Similar to the proxy, it checks for a valid session and redirects the browser to the SSO server if the user is unauthenticated.
Application-Initiated Redirection:
The target application itself is designed to check for an authentication cookie or header on every page load.
If a session is absent, the application code itself triggers a redirect to the SSO Identity Provider (IdP) login page. 




